Task 12 — Final Executive Summary (150–200 words)

At approximately 09:05 AM an administrative employee received a spear-phishing email from a look-alike domain impersonating IT Support. The message used urgency and authority to induce credential submission at 09:12 AM and approval of an unexpected MFA push at 09:14 AM. Telemetry later detected an anomalous-location login at 10:05 AM, which the user confirmed was not intentional. By 10:20 AM multiple employees reported inaccessible shared network folders exhibiting unusual naming conventions.  

The evidence suggests credential harvesting followed by unauthorized account access and subsequent shared-folder impact. Ransomware encryption, data exfiltration, and EHR access remain unconfirmed and require further investigation. Primary organizational priorities are immediate session revocation, credential reset, isolation of affected shares, and preservation of forensic artifacts. Lessons learned include the need for stronger MFA controls, improved email authentication, and reinforced user reporting of unexpected authentication prompts.  
